      "data": {
        "text": "**Defining OAuth Flow**\n\nI'm focusing on crafting a Python script, `start_oauth_login.py`, to kick off an OAuth or OIDC authorization-code login. I've decided to use Google as a concrete provider to make things easier. I need to keep in mind that I'm implementing just the beginning of this process.\n\n\n**Constructing Authorization URL**\n\nI've moved on to the next stage and have decided on Google as my OAuth provider. I'm now crafting the authorization URL within my Python script, `start_oauth_login.py`. I'm focusing on assembling the essential parameters: client ID, redirect URI, response type (code), scopes, access type (offline), state, and prompt (consent) for the URL construction. I'll be using `https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth` as the Google endpoint.\n\n\n**Initiating Script Design**\n\nI'm now diving into the Python script's design, specifically `start_oauth_login.py`. I'm solidifying the constants for Google's authorization endpoint, client ID, redirect URI, and scopes. Placeholders are in place for the `client_id` and `redirect_uri`. The goal now is to create a function, `start_oauth_login`, which will construct and return the authorization URL, streamlining the initial setup.\n\n\n",
